  <h1>Functional Analysis and Variational Methods</h1>
  <p class="lede">Functional analysis extends linear algebra and calculus from finite vectors to function spaces. Variational methods turn operator equations into minimization, stationarity, and weak-form problems.</p>
  <p>This subject clarifies the continuum objects that finite basis sets approximate. It is an advanced support layer rather than a prerequisite for every first DFT calculation: finite-dimensional Rayleigh–Ritz reasoning can be learned first, then strengthened with Hilbert-space domains, convergence, compactness, and spectral theory.</p>

  <h2>Wavefunctions live in spaces with structure</h2>
  <p>A Hilbert space is a complete inner-product space. Its norm is induced by the inner product,</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="norm psi squared equals inner product psi psi">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msup><mrow><mo>∥</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>⟨</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\psi\|^2=\langle\psi|\psi\rangle.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Completeness means that every Cauchy sequence converges to an element of the space. This matters because numerical sequences can converge in one norm but not another, and pointwise convergence need not preserve derivatives, kinetic energy, density, or boundary conditions.</p>
  <p>Different observables naturally involve different function spaces. Square-integrability alone may be insufficient for a kinetic-energy expectation; Sobolev regularity and operator domains determine whether derivatives and quadratic forms are well defined.</p>

  <h2>Operators are defined by both their action and their domain</h2>
  <p>An unbounded operator such as the kinetic-energy operator is not specified only by a differential expression. Its domain and boundary conditions affect self-adjointness and the spectrum.</p>
  <p>For a self-adjoint Hamiltonian, expectation values are real and spectral theory provides the mathematical basis for bound-state expansions, continuous spectra, and projection-valued decompositions. A Hermitian matrix obtained after discretization approximates this structure but does not automatically reproduce every property of the continuum operator.</p>

  <h2>The Rayleigh quotient connects spectra to optimization</h2>
  <p>For any nonzero trial state in the operator domain, the operator-form Rayleigh quotient is</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Rayleigh quotient of psi equals expectation H divided by norm squared">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>|</mo><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[\psi]=\frac&#123;\langle\psi|\hat&#123;H&#125;|\psi\rangle&#125;&#123;\langle\psi|\psi\rangle&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>For a self-adjoint Hamiltonian bounded from below, the infimum over an admissible domain gives the bottom of the spectrum. A semibounded Hamiltonian can be treated more generally through its closed quadratic form on the form domain; in that formulation the numerator is the quadratic-form value rather than an assumed operator expectation. The spectral infimum is a ground-state eigenvalue only when it is attained by an admissible state.</p>
  <p>Restricting the search to a finite trial subspace produces the Rayleigh–Ritz approximation. Enlarging nested trial spaces can lower the approximate ground-state energy toward the continuum limit. This upper-bound statement applies to the variational energy in the declared Hamiltonian and trial class; it does not imply monotonic convergence of every density, eigenvalue difference, force, response function, or post-processed observable.</p>

  <h2>Stationarity under constraints introduces multipliers</h2>
  <p>Orthonormal orbitals are obtained by varying an energy while enforcing overlap constraints. Schematically,</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="variation of energy minus lambda overlap constraint equals zero">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>&#123;</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#125;</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>−</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo></mo></munderover><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta[E[\&#123;\phi_i\&#125;]-\sum_&#123;ij&#125;\lambda_&#123;ij&#125;(\langle\phi_i|\phi_j\rangle-\delta_&#123;ij&#125;)]=0.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The multiplier matrix can often be diagonalized within the occupied subspace, yielding canonical orbital equations. The physically meaningful variational object is usually the occupied subspace or density matrix, not one unique set of canonical orbitals.</p>

  <h2>Functional derivatives require a declared topology</h2>
  <p>A directional variation of a functional can be written</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="delta F equals integral functional derivative times delta n">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>r</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta F[n]=\int\frac&#123;\delta F&#125;&#123;\delta n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)&#125;\delta n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\,d^3r.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Gâteaux and Fréchet differentiability are not identical notions. The result depends on the admissible variations, norm, boundary conditions, constraints, and whether the functional is differentiable at the point considered.</p>
  <p>In density-functional theory, representability and differentiability can fail in ways hidden by finite grids and basis sets. Formal Euler equations should therefore not be read as proof that every trial density or approximate functional satisfies the required mathematical assumptions.</p>

  <h2>Weak formulations move derivatives onto test functions</h2>
  <p>Finite-element and related methods often replace a strong differential equation by an integral identity valid for all test functions in a declared space. This lowers regularity requirements and incorporates boundary conditions systematically.</p>
  <p>A converged discrete weak solution is still a solution of a finite problem. Mesh refinement, polynomial order, quadrature, domain truncation, and boundary treatment must be tested against the target observable.</p>

  <h2>Existence, uniqueness, and stationarity are separate questions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A stationary point need not be a minimum.</li>
    <li>A minimum may be nonunique because of degeneracy or symmetry.</li>
    <li>A minimizing sequence may fail to converge strongly without compactness or coercivity.</li>
    <li>A discretized minimum can converge to the wrong continuum problem if domains or boundary conditions drift.</li>
    <li>Nonlinear approximate functionals can possess multiple local stationary solutions.</li>
  </ul>

